1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,167
Kick a ball up a slope.

2
00:00:02,167 --> 00:00:03,572
It rolls back down.

3
00:00:03,572 --> 00:00:06,285
A waterfall drops and turns a mill wheel.

4
00:00:06,285 --> 00:00:09,456
A solar lamp charges all
day and glows at night.

5
00:00:09,856 --> 00:00:12,638
Energy goes up, then
comes down and does work.

6
00:00:12,638 --> 00:00:14,931
A leaf does the same thing with light.

7
00:00:15,381 --> 00:00:17,387
Diya held a leaf up to the sun.

8
00:00:17,387 --> 00:00:21,479
She asked: light cannot be held in
your hand, so how does a leaf catch

9
00:00:21,479 --> 00:00:22,379
it?

10
00:00:21,883 --> 00:00:23,740
And where does that energy go?

11
00:00:24,090 --> 00:00:25,657
Here is the short answer.

12
00:00:25,657 --> 00:00:28,923
Pigments catch light and push
electrons up to high energy.

13
00:00:28,923 --> 00:00:32,772
As the electrons come down, step
by step, their energy is stored.

14
00:00:33,272 --> 00:00:36,966
By the end, you will know the light
reaction, the two photosystems, the

15
00:00:36,966 --> 00:00:39,034
Z scheme, and the splitting of water.

16
00:00:39,684 --> 00:00:41,748
First, what is the light reaction?

17
00:00:42,098 --> 00:00:45,402
It is the part of photosynthesis
that needs light directly.

18
00:00:45,402 --> 00:00:47,585
It happens on the thylakoid membranes.

19
00:00:48,035 --> 00:00:51,982
It has four events: light is absorbed,
water is split, oxygen is released,

20
00:00:51,982 --> 00:00:54,737
and two energy carriers,
ATP and NADPH, are made.

21
00:00:55,387 --> 00:00:57,474
Let's zoom in to where light is caught.

22
00:00:57,824 --> 00:01:01,635
Inside a chloroplast, the thylakoid
membranes hold groups of pigments.

23
00:01:02,135 --> 00:01:03,144
Zoom closer.

24
00:01:03,144 --> 00:01:07,407
Each group is a light harvesting
complex, inside a photosystem.

25
00:01:07,407 --> 00:01:11,359
Most of its pigments act like
an antenna, catching light.

26
00:01:11,859 --> 00:01:13,328
Zoom to the centre.

27
00:01:13,328 --> 00:01:17,573
The antenna passes the energy to
just one chlorophyll a molecule.

28
00:01:17,573 --> 00:01:19,563
This is the reaction centre.

29
00:01:20,213 --> 00:01:24,041
Now follow one electron
along its path, step by step.

30
00:01:24,391 --> 00:01:25,291
Step one.

31
00:01:25,133 --> 00:01:28,933
Photosystem two absorbs red light
of six hundred eighty nanometres.

32
00:01:28,933 --> 00:01:32,425
An electron in its reaction
centre jumps up to high energy.

33
00:01:33,175 --> 00:01:34,075
Step two.

34
00:01:33,792 --> 00:01:37,355
An electron acceptor catches it and
passes it down a chain of cytochromes.

35
00:01:37,355 --> 00:01:38,966
Cytochromes are carrier proteins.

36
00:01:38,966 --> 00:01:40,819
This is the electron transport chain.

37
00:01:41,569 --> 00:01:42,469
Step three.

38
00:01:42,425 --> 00:01:44,657
The electron reaches photosystem one.

39
00:01:45,407 --> 00:01:46,307
Step four.

40
00:01:46,185 --> 00:01:49,429
Photosystem one absorbs light
of seven hundred nanometres.

41
00:01:49,429 --> 00:01:52,692
Its electron jumps up again,
to an even higher energy.

42
00:01:53,442 --> 00:01:54,342
Step five.

43
00:01:54,167 --> 00:01:56,910
It moves down to NADP
plus, which becomes NADPH.

44
00:01:56,910 --> 00:02:00,304
Drawn on an energy scale, this
path looks like the letter Z.

45
00:02:00,304 --> 00:02:02,072
So it is called the Z scheme.

46
00:02:02,822 --> 00:02:03,738
Remember this.

47
00:02:03,738 --> 00:02:05,229
Up, down, up, down.

48
00:02:05,229 --> 00:02:06,154
The Z scheme.

49
00:02:06,754 --> 00:02:07,654
But wait.

50
00:02:07,596 --> 00:02:10,172
Photosystem two gave away an electron.

51
00:02:10,172 --> 00:02:11,383
Who refills it?

52
00:02:11,733 --> 00:02:12,633
Water does.

53
00:02:12,563 --> 00:02:15,196
Water splitting is linked
to photosystem two.

54
00:02:15,196 --> 00:02:19,271
Two water molecules split into four
protons, one oxygen molecule and four

55
00:02:19,271 --> 00:02:20,171
electrons.

56
00:02:20,451 --> 00:02:22,729
These electrons refill photosystem two.

57
00:02:22,729 --> 00:02:24,979
The oxygen is released into the air.

58
00:02:24,979 --> 00:02:26,901
That is the oxygen we breathe.

59
00:02:27,551 --> 00:02:28,962
Now compare the two photosystems.

60
00:02:29,312 --> 00:02:30,319
Photosystem two.

61
00:02:30,319 --> 00:02:34,399
Its reaction centre is P six eighty,
because it absorbs best at six hundred

62
00:02:34,399 --> 00:02:35,493
eighty nanometres.

63
00:02:35,493 --> 00:02:37,481
It is linked with splitting water.

64
00:02:37,931 --> 00:02:38,973
Photosystem one.

65
00:02:38,973 --> 00:02:42,911
Its reaction centre is P seven hundred,
absorbing best at seven hundred

66
00:02:42,911 --> 00:02:43,811
nanometres.

67
00:02:43,677 --> 00:02:45,691
It passes electrons to NADP plus.

68
00:02:46,341 --> 00:02:48,643
Here is a strange fact about the names.

69
00:02:48,993 --> 00:02:51,993
The photosystems were numbered in the
order they were discovered, not in

70
00:02:51,993 --> 00:02:52,993
the order they work.

71
00:02:53,443 --> 00:02:57,893
So photosystem two works first,
and photosystem one works second.

72
00:02:58,493 --> 00:02:59,393
Your turn.

73
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:03,013
Which photosystem is linked with
splitting water, one or two?

74
00:03:03,013 --> 00:03:04,201
Pause and decide.

75
00:03:07,401 --> 00:03:08,386
Photosystem two!

76
00:03:08,386 --> 00:03:10,759
Its lost electrons are
refilled from water.

77
00:03:11,409 --> 00:03:12,913
Now, a worked example.

78
00:03:12,913 --> 00:03:15,466
Count the electrons for
one oxygen molecule.

79
00:03:15,816 --> 00:03:19,532
Splitting two water molecules gives
one oxygen molecule and four electrons.

80
00:03:19,982 --> 00:03:22,012
Each NADP plus needs two
electrons to become NADPH.

81
00:03:22,462 --> 00:03:24,525
Four electrons divided by two is two.

82
00:03:24,525 --> 00:03:27,136
So one oxygen released
goes with two NADPH made.

83
00:03:27,786 --> 00:03:29,248
Now, the exam trap.

84
00:03:29,598 --> 00:03:33,997
Many students think photosystem one
works first, because it is number one.

85
00:03:34,397 --> 00:03:37,173
But the numbers show
the order of discovery.

86
00:03:37,173 --> 00:03:40,090
In the Z scheme,
photosystem two works first.

87
00:03:40,740 --> 00:03:42,127
Where is this asked?

88
00:03:42,127 --> 00:03:46,531
As assertion reason and statement
questions on P six eighty, P seven hundred

89
00:03:46,531 --> 00:03:47,863
and water splitting.

90
00:03:48,463 --> 00:03:49,594
Exam level now.

91
00:03:49,594 --> 00:03:51,810
Read the assertion and the reason.

92
00:03:51,810 --> 00:03:52,990
Pause and solve.

93
00:03:58,390 --> 00:03:59,452
The answer is A.

94
00:03:59,452 --> 00:04:02,366
Both are true, and the reason
explains the assertion.

95
00:04:02,866 --> 00:04:07,160
Photosystem two loses electrons every
time light hits its reaction centre.

96
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:09,032
So it needs a steady supply.

97
00:04:09,532 --> 00:04:12,577
Water splitting gives electrons
that replace the lost ones.

98
00:04:12,577 --> 00:04:14,580
So the reason explains the assertion.

99
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:16,720
Here is your recap.

100
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:18,302
Screenshot this card.

101
00:04:21,502 --> 00:04:23,688
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

102
00:04:24,088 --> 00:04:24,988
One.

103
00:04:24,539 --> 00:04:26,175
What is a reaction centre?

104
00:04:27,075 --> 00:04:27,975
Two.

105
00:04:27,483 --> 00:04:29,219
Why is it called the Z scheme?

106
00:04:30,119 --> 00:04:31,019
Three.

107
00:04:30,659 --> 00:04:33,740
Which photosystem splits
water, and what is released?

108
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:37,794
Next time: how does a pile of protons
spin a tiny machine that makes ATP?

109
00:04:37,794 --> 00:04:39,795
We find out in photophosphorylation
and chemiosmosis.
